AI Data Analysis Agent

A chat-based AI agent, built in [n8n](https://n8n.io), that lets you upload a
CSV/Excel file and ask questions about it in plain English. It plans the
analysis with an LLM, performs the actual math deterministically in code, and
replies with clear, business-ready answers, tables, and insights.

---

## 1. Project Overview

What it does — You upload a spreadsheet (CSV / XLSX / XLS) into a chat, then
ask natural-language questions ("What is the total revenue?", "Which product
line has the highest profit margin?", "Show outliers in revenue"). The agent
analyzes your data and answers with numbers, tables, and short business
insights.

Why it was built — Non-technical users often have data in spreadsheets but
no fast way to interrogate it without writing formulas, pivot tables, or SQL.
This agent turns everyday questions into correct calculations without the user
touching a formula.

Who it is for — Analysts, operators, founders, and business users who want
quick, trustworthy answers from their own datasets, and anyone learning how to
combine LLM planning with deterministic computation in n8n.

---

## 2. Problem Statement

- Manually analyzing CSV/Excel files is slow and error-prone (pivot tables,
  lookups, hand-written formulas).
- People think in questions ("which region grew the most?"), not in
  spreadsheet operations.
- Real analysis needs **calculations, comparisons, rankings, and insights**,
  not just a chat model guessing numbers — LLMs are unreliable at arithmetic.

---

## 3. Solution

An AI agent that separates thinking from calculating:

```
Upload dataset → ask a question → AI plans the analysis →
code performs the exact calculations → AI explains the results
```

The LLM decides what to compute (which operation, which columns). A code
step does the actual arithmetic over your real rows, so the numbers are always
correct and reproducible. A second LLM step turns those computed numbers into a
readable answer — it is explicitly forbidden from inventing or changing any
figure.

---

## 4. Key Features

- CSV / Excel analysis — `.csv`, `.xlsx`, `.xls`
- Natural-language questions — no formulas or query language needed
- Calculations — sum, average, count, distinct count, min, max, median
- Filtering and grouping — break a metric down by one or two categories
- Comparisons — period-over-period / trend (year, quarter, month, day) with
  change and % change
- Ranking — top-N / bottom-N groups
- Derived metrics — margin, rate, ratio, percentage, average-per-X,
  computed correctly **per group** (never by ranking the numerator alone)
- Descriptive statistics — count, min, max, range, mean, median, mode,
  std dev, variance, Q1/Q3, IQR, skewness, custom percentiles
- Distributions — value counts for categories; a proper histogram for
  numeric columns (contiguous, non-overlapping bins, each value counted once)
- Outliers — IQR method, returns the actual offending rows with bounds
- Extremes — the actual record(s) with the highest/lowest value
- Correlation — Pearson between two numeric columns
- Multi-step / complex questions — decomposed into ordered steps; later
  steps are scoped to the subgroup an earlier step identified
- Business insights — cautious, evidence-based interpretation
- Session-based Q&A — remembers your uploaded dataset for the conversation
- Structured responses — Answer / Results (tables) / Key Insight

---

## 5. System Architecture

```
        ┌──────┐
        │ User │  (chat: uploads file + asks questions)
        └──┬───┘
           │
     ┌─────▼───────────────┐
     │ n8n Chat Trigger    │  file upload + message
     └─────┬───────────────┘
           │
     ┌─────▼───────────────┐
     │ Data Processing     │  detect file type, parse CSV/XLSX/XLS,
     │ (parse + profile)   │  profile columns (type, stats)
     └─────┬───────────────┘
           │
     ┌─────▼───────────────┐        ┌─────────────────────────┐
     │ Data Q&A Session    │◄──────►│ Data QA Sessions table  │
     │ store / retrieve    │        │ (dataset + profile per  │
     └─────┬───────────────┘        │  session)               │
           │                        └─────────────────────────┘
     ┌─────▼───────────────┐
     │ AI Agent (planner)  │  LLM converts question → analysis plan
     └─────┬───────────────┘
           │
     ┌─────▼───────────────┐
     │ Calculation engine  │  code node runs the exact math on real rows
     └─────┬───────────────┘
           │
     ┌─────▼───────────────┐
     │ AI Explainer        │  LLM formats results (no number invention)
     └─────┬───────────────┘
           │
     ┌─────▼───────────────┐
     │ Final Response      │  Answer / Results / Key Insight
     └─────────────────────┘
```

---

## 6. Technology Stack

| Layer | Technology |
|---|---|
| Orchestration | n8n (workflow automation) |
| AI / LLM | Google Gemini (`models/gemini-3.1-flash-lite`) via the Google Gemini (PaLM) API credential |
| File processing | n8n Extract From File (CSV / XLSX / XLS) |
| Calculations | n8n Code node (deterministic JavaScript) |
| Session storage | n8n Data Table ("Data QA Sessions") |
| Version control / delivery | GitHub |

---

## 7. How the Agent Works

### Step 1: Upload dataset
The user uploads a CSV/XLSX/XLS file in the chat.

### Step 2: Dataset processing
The workflow detects the file type, parses it into rows, and builds a profile
— for each column it detects the type (numeric / text / boolean / date) and,
for numeric columns, basic stats. The dataset + profile are stored in the
Data QA Sessions table, keyed to the chat session. The agent confirms the
detected rows and columns.

### Step 3: User asks a question
The user asks in plain English. The stored dataset is reused for the whole
conversation.

### Step 4: AI interprets the question
An LLM (the planner) converts the question into an ordered plan of analytical
steps — choosing the operation(s), columns, filters, grouping, and ordering.
It does not compute numbers itself.

### Step 5: Data is analyzed
A Code node executes the plan against the real rows: aggregates, group-bys,
top-N, derived metrics, comparisons, statistics, distributions, outliers,
extremes, correlations — including scoping later steps to the subgroup an
earlier step identified.

### Step 6: Calculation / validation
All arithmetic happens in code, so results are exact and reproducible.
Guardrails: no silent column substitution, correct per-group derived metrics,
single-assignment histogram bins, IQR outlier detection.

### Step 7: Final answer
A second LLM formats the computed results into Answer / Results (tables) /
Key Insight using only the calculated values verbatim — it cannot invent or
change a number.

---

## 8. Setup & Installation

### Prerequisites
- An n8n instance (cloud or self-hosted) that supports the LangChain / AI nodes
  and Data Tables.
- A Google Gemini API key (Google AI Studio).

### Import the n8n workflow
1. In n8n, go to Workflows → Import from File
2. Select `data-qa.workflow.json` from this repo.

### Configure API credentials
1. Create a Google Gemini (PaLM) API credential in n8n with your own API key.
2. Open the imported workflow and select that credential on both model nodes:
   Intent Model and Explain Model
   > The exported workflow contains only a credential *reference* (id + name),
   > never an API key — you must supply your own.

### Configure the Data Q&A session table
1. Create a Data Table named Data QA Sessions with these columns
   (n8n normalizes names to snake_case):
   - `session_id` (string)
   - `file_name` (string)
   - `row_count` (number)
   - `col_count` (number)
   - `profile_json` (string)
   - `dataset_json` (string)
2. On the Store Session and Get Session nodes, point the Data Table
   field at your table.

### Configure required variables / settings
- No environment variables are required. The only per-instance settings are the
  Gemini credential and the Data Table selection above.

### Activate / run the workflow
- For testing, open the workflow and click Open chat (the chat trigger is
  private by default).
- Publish the workflow when you're ready to use it regularly.

---

## 9. Configuration

| Item | Value / Notes |
|---|---|
| Required API key | Google Gemini API key (yours) |
| Credential type | Google Gemini (PaLM) API, used by Intent Model and Explain Model |
| Model | `models/gemini-3.1-flash-lite` |
| Session table | Data Table **Data QA Sessions** with the columns listed in §8 |
| Chat trigger | Private by default (`public: false`) — use the in-editor Open chat; make public only if you want a shareable URL |

---

## 10. Tradeoffs & Design Decisions

- Why n8n — fast to build, visual, easy to import/share, and it provides
  file parsing, chat, LLM, and data-table primitives out of the box.
- Why an AI agent — natural language removes the need for formulas/SQL and
  handles the huge variety of ways people phrase questions.
- Why a session table — the dataset must persist across multiple questions
  in a conversation without re-uploading; storing dataset + profile keyed by
  session enables that.
- Why an external calculation layer (code node) — LLMs are unreliable at
  arithmetic. Doing the math in code makes results exact, reproducible, and
  auditable; the LLM only plans and explains.

---

## 11. Known Limitations

- Manual API credential configuration — each user must add their own Gemini
  credential and select it on the two model nodes.
- Manual session-table setup — the Data Table must be created and linked
  once per instance.
- Supported file formats — CSV, XLSX, XLS only.
- Data-size limits — the dataset is stored and processed in the workflow;
  very large files may hit memory/time limits. Best for small-to-medium files
  (roughly up to tens of thousands of rows).
- Edge cases — messy headers, mixed-type columns, or unusual date formats
  can affect type detection; ambiguous questions trigger a clarification prompt.
- Session lifetime — a dataset lives per chat session; a new conversation
  needs a new upload.

---

## 12. Future Improvements

- Stronger calculation validation / self-checks
- Automated data-quality checks on upload (missing values, type conflicts)
- More file/data sources (Google Sheets, databases, JSON, APIs)
- Better ambiguity handling and follow-up questions
- Automated session-table creation on first run
- Chart / visualization generation alongside tables

---

## 13. Reproducibility

Steps a reviewer can follow:

1. Import `data-qa.workflow.json` into an n8n instance.
2. Create a Google Gemini credential and select it on Intent Model and
   Explain Model
3. Create the Data QA Sessions Data Table and link it on
   Store Session / Get Session.
4. Open the workflow, click Open chat, and upload the sample file
5. Ask the analytical & business realted questions in the chat box.
6. Expected behavior — the agent confirms the detected rows/columns on
   upload, then returns Answer / Results / Key Insight for each question, with
   numbers computed from the data (not invented) and multi-step questions scoped
   correctly to the identified subgroup.

---

## 14. Project Status

- Current version — v1, Gemini-powered (`models/gemini-3.1-flash-lite`).
- What has been tested — end-to-end live chat: file upload + profiling,
  aggregates, grouping, top-N, derived metrics (per-group), comparisons,
  statistics, distributions (contiguous bins), IQR outliers, extremes, and
  multi-step subgroup-scoped questions.
- Production-ready vs experimental — the core analysis engine is stable and
  tested; treat large-file handling, unusual date parsing, and highly ambiguous
  questions as areas still being hardened.



